       <narrative xml:lang="EN">&lt;p&amp;gt;&lt;strong&amp;gt;RI achieved.&lt;/strong&amp;gt; The target was met&amp;mdash;the share of new PATH beneficiaries from rural and peri-urban areas increased to above 85 percent.&amp;nbsp; However, while CCT spending increased from J$8.0 billion in FY2019/20 to J$13.3 billion in FY2021/22, this increase was in part attributed to additional funding related to the COVID-19 pandemic. A prior action under the COVID emergency response operation aimed to mitigate the pandemic&amp;rsquo;s impact on the poor, with the GOJ making a one-off additional payment to PATH beneficiary households, as well as and through a Social Pension for individuals over 75 years old not in receipt of other pensions. Nonetheless, the PATH funding levels are budgeted to J$10.9 billion for 2023/24 -&amp;nbsp; higher than the baseline. This suggests that the increases in PATH benefits are sustained, especially given that (i) the unemployment rate reached historically low rate of 4.5 percent in April 2023, and (ii) the national poverty rate is estimated to have declined to 12.6 in 2022 and projected to decline to or below pre-pandemic levels by 2024.&lt;/p&amp;gt;

